hi there steve kaufman  today i want to talk about  which language is the best language or  which languages are the best languages  uh people often ask me  uh you know should i learn this language  or that language  uh  and but what really triggered this video  was  uh i'm sort of getting tired of having  russians come on  here commenting on my youtube channel or  in  in twitter  and telling me in all seriousness that  ukrainian is just some kind of a funny  dialect of russian  uh when russians hear ukrainian it  sounds funny  uh ukrainians uh can't really i mean  these are actual quotes ukrainians the  language doesn't really exist they can't  really communicate with each other  ukrainian they speak russian to each  other and on and on  um  my experience has been that ukrainian is  very much a language on its own  uh vocabulary more similar to polish  if anything  if i look at polish now that i've  started polish if i look at polish  ukrainian czech and russian  in terms of vocabulary the  russian is the outlier  which doesn't matter i mean each  language developed has its own history  and so forth and so on that those  languages and the belarus and probably  the south slavic languages were all part  of a  original  you know proto-slavic language at some  point or even before that in a  proto-european language sure so what  uh to my mind the answer what is the  best language they're all equally good  definitely  and um they're equally good at  expressing  things they do it in different ways  but you hear a lot of this sort of  you know french is precise the japanese  is vague  but  if japanese person wants to be precise  they can be precise they may take a few  more words  to do so uh but they can achieve it i  have you know i speak a lot of languages  i've often been in a situation of  interpreting i have never and this is  interpreting from chinese to english to  french japanese whatever i have never  found that i'm not able to express  a certain thought in another language  all languages work  and  the number of speakers again i don't  want to pick on the russians but i was  telling some russians that i was  learning czech and so why would they  reaction why would you possibly learn  czech there's only 10 million people and  it's you know insignificant that you  know our russian language is one of the  greatest languages and all this kind of  stuff it's not it's just one language  and so is czech and so is finnish and so  is the language spoken by the inuit  and spoken by the uh  i don't know some tribe in the jungle  um  languages have different evolutions it  may be that the  total amount of say literature produced  in one language is greater than produced  in another language  fine  some of the great works  creative works were never written down  you know the works of homer  uh were part of an oral tradition for  centuries the ramayana epic  in india and southeast asia  which is very popular today was just  a story that was told by people i'm sure  that the  the inuit in the far north and the  and  people wherever you find them  who  have oral traditions and haven't yet  written things down will all have  wonderful  works of literature  i've heard i have no indirect experience  that  the languages of the native indians in  north america are very complex they're  not sort of simple because those people  haven't built you know  castles and cities and  all of the  sorts of things that we identify with  quote civilization uh they had a  different lifestyle hunting lifestyle  whatever it doesn't mean that their  language is less complex less  capable of expressing things and so  forth at least insofar as the languages  that i have come across  uh every language that i learn  like now starting with polish  it's wonderful i enjoy it and  there's there's often prejudice against  certain languages like  well russians versus these are the  ukrainian for example or  uh chinese mandarin speakers who  consider you know cantonese like i was  in beijing once and  told them that i uh spoke uh cantonese  their term for cantonese they call it  nyoyu because to them the sounds sound  so like bird chirping whatever it might  be  people say that dutch is an ugly  language i haven't started dutch but  i'll tell you if i start learning dutch  i'm sure i'm going to enjoy it  some people say that danish you know  sounds like  people speaking with hot potatoes in  their mouth or something  um  i i love listening to danish and  i really enjoyed watching this movie  babette's feast if you're a language  lover you gotta watch it because there  you can hear swedish danish  and italian  and it's a lovely movie  uh  danish i love listening to danish  cantonese  i must say before i started it i thought  it didn't sound as nice as mandarin but  once i get into it  i enjoy it just as much so  you know to me there's no hierarchy of  of languages  they're all equally  uh valid equally beautiful equally  expressive  uh and the only thing that matters is  which one are you motivated to learn as  i said about accents which accent are  you motivated to imitate  uh language is just a means of  communication  uh there are no hierarchies i uh i'm  often amused by the sort of cultural  chauvinism that certain people seem to  express again the chinese we have five  thousand years of history no one no  chinese person has ever told me what was  that event five thousand years ago  from which they count their five  thousand years there was no event  nothing particularly important happened  five thousand years ago it's just  we have five thousand years of history  okay everybody has history the chinese  evolve one way i mean we all have a  common grandmother apparently some sixty  thousand years ago and we all evolved  our separate different ways  uh  and i must say i just find that any form  of cultural chauvinism  linguistic chauvinism  robs me the wrong way  yeah i enjoyed learning czech 10 million  people if i were to go after finnish  that's why i like moses mccormick like  he studies georgian and estonian and  i don't know hmong or some language from  southeast asia it's all part of that  human experience that we can explore  and i don't see any purpose in  trying to create hierarchies of better  or worse more important less important  more beautiful less beautiful more  expressive less expressive i haven't  found that to be the case at least with  the 15 or 16 languages that i have  either learned or tried to learn so  that's my take on the best language  in the world the best language to study  is the one you're most motivated to  learn  thank you for listening

